1
00:00:00,256 --> 00:00:04,617
Here is our small arch in Blender, and here it is inside the
woodland.

2
00:00:04,779 --> 00:00:09,818
We can walk up to it and look around its sides. This advanced
lesson follows the files between

3
00:00:09,851 --> 00:00:15,133
those two views, using eight simple boxes and a native asset
import that we've checked in the game.

4
00:00:20,862 --> 00:00:27,151
We'll open the supplied starter, change one useful dimension,
export a box list and convert it into a native

5
00:00:27,176 --> 00:00:32,936
asset file. Then we'll import into an empty practice scene before
adding it to a copy of our woodland.

6
00:00:33,046 --> 00:00:36,056
Keep the restored challenge from the previous lesson available.

7
00:00:36,937 --> 00:00:40,872
This route recreates explicit boxes as native game objects.

8
00:00:40,991 --> 00:00:43,764
It isn't a general importer for every Blender mesh.

9
00:00:43,916 --> 00:00:49,664
The supplied helper checks for simple box geometry, positive
dimensions and zero object rotation.

10
00:00:49,847 --> 00:00:55,616
Textures, rigs, animation and arbitrary F B X models are outside
this example.

11
00:00:55,777 --> 00:00:58,236
Start with this supported shape before adapting it.

12
00:00:58,929 --> 00:01:04,933
A blend file is the editable Blender project. Our JSON file lists
the boxes, positions,

13
00:01:05,030 --> 00:01:10,637
sizes and colours. The converter writes a G I A file that the game
can import as assets.

14
00:01:10,662 --> 00:01:13,917
Renaming a blend file doesn't perform that conversion.

15
00:01:14,039 --> 00:01:18,329
Keep these three stages distinct so you know which file each
application needs.

16
00:01:19,052 --> 00:01:24,522
In Blender, use File, Open, and choose Woodland Arch Start from
the resources.

17
00:01:24,684 --> 00:01:31,582
The recorded version is Blender four point two L T S. In the
Outliner, find the Miliastra Boxes collection.

18
00:01:31,658 --> 00:01:38,935
It contains two feet, two posts, two caps, a beam and a plaque.
Those eight objects make the complete prop.

19
00:01:39,652 --> 00:01:45,296
Stay in Object Mode. Select the object named E P zero nine Beam in
the Outliner,

20
00:01:45,351 --> 00:01:50,971
then inspect its Transform values. The sidebar's Dimensions
describe the object's bounding size.

21
00:01:51,025 --> 00:01:54,499
Location sets its position, while Scale is a multiplier.

22
00:01:54,584 --> 00:01:59,542
Read the selected object's name before editing, so the dimension
change affects the beam.

23
00:02:00,323 --> 00:02:05,986
Change the beam's X dimension from four point four to four point
eight, then look at the front view.

24
00:02:06,126 --> 00:02:12,065
The crosspiece becomes a little wider while the posts stay where
they were. This is an example design choice.

25
00:02:12,139 --> 00:02:17,667
Your useful check is that the beam still spans both posts and the
change appears on the intended object.

26
00:02:19,380 --> 00:02:24,137
For this helper, keep each object's rotation at zero and its
dimensions positive.

27
00:02:24,352 --> 00:02:29,289
Leave the box meshes centred on their object origins, without
parenting or modifiers.

28
00:02:29,387 --> 00:02:34,887
The exporter reads the resulting dimensions directly, so this
lesson doesn't require an Apply Scale step.

29
00:02:34,992 --> 00:02:38,912
A bevel or a rotated mesh would require a different conversion
design.

30
00:02:39,818 --> 00:02:44,094
Use File, Save As, and give your edited project a new name.

31
00:02:44,213 --> 00:02:48,178
We keep Woodland Arch Start and Woodland Arch Final as separate
files.

32
00:02:48,328 --> 00:02:52,782
Save the Blender project before making the external export. That
preserves an editable

33
00:02:52,807 --> 00:02:57,733
model you can return to, instead of relying on a generated asset
file as your only copy.

34
00:02:58,448 --> 00:03:04,900
Open the Scripting workspace and select the embedded text named
Export Miliastra Boxes dot P Y.

35
00:03:04,976 --> 00:03:09,337
Review the supplied source, then use the Text Editor's Run Script
button.

36
00:03:09,456 --> 00:03:14,496
This registers our session's export menu. The shown closeup is a
read-only reference.

37
00:03:14,636 --> 00:03:19,656
Reopening Blender may require running the helper again; we haven't
installed it as a permanent add-on.

38
00:03:20,309 --> 00:03:24,889
Return to File, Export, and choose Miliastra Boxes, JSON.

39
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:31,298
Choose a new filename and read the Game Origin fields before
exporting.

40
00:03:31,438 --> 00:03:34,613
Our resources contain the exact exported lists.

41
00:03:34,638 --> 00:03:40,672
The file browser is shown only through necessary field crops,
keeping unrelated folders outside the lesson.

42
00:03:40,757 --> 00:03:45,094
This export describes eight supported boxes; it doesn't write the
game asset yet.

43
00:03:45,742 --> 00:03:51,246
Blender uses Z for the vertical direction here. Our game placement
list uses Y for up.

44
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:56,976
The helper maps Blender X, Y, Z into game X, Z, negative Y,

45
00:03:57,074 --> 00:04:02,838
then adds the Game Origin offset. It also reorders the dimensions.
This is why copying three

46
00:04:02,862 --> 00:04:07,838
position numbers directly between the applications can turn a
sensible prop the wrong way.

47
00:04:08,623 --> 00:04:14,094
Game Origin moves the whole exported arrangement without changing
the distances between its boxes.

48
00:04:14,170 --> 00:04:21,214
We first used eight, zero, sixteen in an empty scene. In the
woodland, that position overlapped a tree.

49
00:04:21,239 --> 00:04:27,542
We exported a separate placement at sixteen, zero, sixteen and
imported it into a fresh copy.

50
00:04:27,682 --> 00:04:31,510
Choose clear space in your own layout; these coordinates are
examples.

51
00:04:32,225 --> 00:04:37,397
The companion Primitive Kit contains the conversion source, its
box template and license.

52
00:04:37,473 --> 00:04:43,405
It uses Python outside the game. Open a terminal in that kit's
folder, create the shown local virtual

53
00:04:43,430 --> 00:04:48,659
environment and install the pinned requirement. This keeps the
helper's package in its own folder.

54
00:04:48,748 --> 00:04:54,518
Our fresh environment check used Python three point fourteen and
protobuf six point thirty-three point six.

55
00:04:55,280 --> 00:05:01,101
Use the environment's Python to run convert boxes dot P Y,
followed by your input JSON and a new

56
00:05:01,126 --> 00:05:06,996
output G I A filename. The command reference uses relative paths
you can copy from the guide.

57
00:05:07,115 --> 00:05:12,074
Run it from the Primitive Kit folder, where the source modules and
Template folder are available.

58
00:05:12,278 --> 00:05:15,650
Choose a fresh output name because existing files are preserved.

59
00:05:16,405 --> 00:05:21,726
The converter reports eight primitives and reads back their names,
transforms and colours.

60
00:05:21,823 --> 00:05:24,542
That helps catch a broken file before opening the game.

61
00:05:24,597 --> 00:05:28,564
A successful structural check still doesn't show what the prop
looks like in a scene.

62
00:05:28,628 --> 00:05:33,998
Keep the output beside its readback report, then move on to the
native import and appearance check.

63
00:05:34,809 --> 00:05:41,653
On Local Save, create a separate practice save using the same
Beyond Mode workflow. Open it and Quick Save.

64
00:05:41,779 --> 00:05:47,378
This empty scene makes a missing box, flipped direction or
unexpected size easier to notice.

65
00:05:47,582 --> 00:05:52,456
It also gives us a clear place to learn the import controls before
changing our completed woodland.

66
00:05:53,143 --> 00:05:56,736
Open System Menu, then Manage Asset Import Export.

67
00:05:56,812 --> 00:06:02,026
Use the Import and Transfer tab and Load External Assets to choose
the generated G I A.

68
00:06:02,102 --> 00:06:08,545
The operating-system file chooser stays outside the captured view.
When the asset pack appears, read its name.

69
00:06:08,727 --> 00:06:14,106
This is an asset import; it isn't the whole-save Import Save
command from the previous lesson.

70
00:06:16,223 --> 00:06:21,352
Select the intended pack and use the transfer control. Read the
native confirmation,

71
00:06:21,428 --> 00:06:26,230
including its undo-history notice, before confirming this change
in your fresh sandbox.

72
00:06:26,255 --> 00:06:32,729
After transfer, the Assets Exporting, Entity view lists all eight
E P zero nine objects.

73
00:06:32,783 --> 00:06:37,543
A transfer message and eight names are useful checks, but we still
need to see the model.

74
00:06:38,252 --> 00:06:44,462
Start a private test. Find the arch, walk closer and turn the
camera to inspect its front and side.

75
00:06:44,666 --> 00:06:49,774
We can see both teal posts, the pale feet and crosspiece, and the
coloured plaque.

76
00:06:49,957 --> 00:06:54,137
Compare those relationships with Blender. This checks this prop's
actual appearance

77
00:06:54,162 --> 00:06:58,385
and orientation, without claiming every Blender model follows the
same route.

78
00:07:03,237 --> 00:07:06,035
Copy the restored woodland into a new lesson card.

79
00:07:06,126 --> 00:07:11,832
Import the separate Placed asset file through the same asset
controls. In this final copy,

80
00:07:11,886 --> 00:07:17,818
the arch sits clear of the tree while the path, pavilion, coins
and interface remain visible.

81
00:07:17,970 --> 00:07:23,014
We leave the earlier overlapping attempt available and use the
clean placement for our final walk.

82
00:07:23,780 --> 00:07:28,248
Walk toward the arch and turn to see its thickness and the space
around its posts.

83
00:07:28,345 --> 00:07:32,575
Keep the view at normal speed. This example's exported boxes are
decorative,

84
00:07:32,629 --> 00:07:36,031
with collision and climbing disabled in the conversion settings.

85
00:07:36,256 --> 00:07:40,923
Don't treat the prop as a working platform without designing and
testing that behaviour separately.

86
00:07:41,050 --> 00:07:44,136
We don't start the timed challenge during this placement check.

87
00:07:45,723 --> 00:07:49,538
Save the final scene and keep the starter blend, edited blend,

88
00:07:49,563 --> 00:07:53,765
exported JSON, generated asset and conversion source together.

89
00:07:53,852 --> 00:08:00,026
The resources identify the files actually imported in these checks
and include a final whole-save export.

90
00:08:00,124 --> 00:08:03,603
That final whole-save file hasn't been separately reimported.

91
00:08:03,694 --> 00:08:07,656
The native appearance evidence belongs to the asset imports we've
just shown.

92
00:08:08,398 --> 00:08:12,938
You now have a woodland scene, recoverable save files, native coin
logic,

93
00:08:12,962 --> 00:08:18,996
a live progress bar, a timed result, achievements and one
supported Blender prop workflow.

94
00:08:19,080 --> 00:08:24,251
Try one small change of your own, then check its effect in private
play. Keep the previous

95
00:08:24,285 --> 00:08:29,096
working version available, and use the guide's official references
when you meet a new module.
